C = [
("h1", "1. How Paul Names the Cross"),
("p", "Claim: Paul does not use one word for what Christ did on the Cross. He says that Christ died, that he was crucified, that he gave himself, that he was delivered, that he was sacrificed, that he shed blood, that he was made a curse and made to be sin. A reader who treats the words as one has already decided that the passages say one thing. A reader who lets each word stand in its sentence finds that the sentences say different things."),
("sub", "1.1 The words and where they stand"),
("p", "The table sets out the main words of the volume with the passages in which Paul uses them. It does not claim that the list is complete. It claims that the words differ, and that each of them sits in a sentence with its own subject, its own object and its own result."),
("tbl", "The words for the Cross in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 15:3", "“Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures”", "Died; for sins; the first thing delivered"),
    ("Rom 5:8", "“while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us”", "Died; for us; while sinners"),
    ("1Cor 2:2", "“Jesus Christ, and him crucified”", "Crucified; the one thing Paul determined to know"),
    ("Gal 1:4", "“Who gave himself for our sins”", "Gave himself; for sins; to deliver from this present evil world"),
    ("Rom 4:25", "“delivered for our offences”", "Delivered; for offences; paired with raised for justification"),
    ("Rom 3:25", "“a propitiation through faith in his blood”", "Set forth by God; propitiation; blood; faith"),
    ("Gal 3:13", "“being made a curse for us”", "Made a curse; for us; redeemed from the curse of the law"),
    ("2Cor 5:21", "“made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin”", "Made sin; for us; that we might be made the righteousness of God"),
    ("1Cor 5:7", "“Christ our passover is sacrificed for us”", "Sacrificed; for us; the passover named"),
    ("Eph 5:2", "“an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweetsmelling savour”", "Given for us; offering and sacrifice; to God"),
    ("Eph 2:16", "“having slain the enmity thereby”", "The cross slays the enmity; both reconciled in one body"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "1.2 What the words are said to do"),
("p", "The verbs and their objects are not the same. Christ “died for the ungodly” (Romans 5:6) and the objects of the death are persons. He “gave himself for our sins” (Galatians 1:4), and the object is sins. He was “made a curse” (Galatians 3:13), and the object is a curse that stood against those under the law. He is “our passover” (1 Corinthians 5:7), and the figure is drawn from a feast. He is “our peace” (Ephesians 2:14), and the figure is drawn from a wall. In none of these is the figure explained by another. The passover is not said to be the curse, and the curse is not said to be the wall."),
("p", "The point is not that the figures conflict. The point is that Paul chooses each to meet a particular need in a particular letter. The letter to the Galatians is written to people who were being told to take up the law, and the curse of the law is the figure that answers them. The letter to the Colossians is written to people who were being offered another fullness, and the record against them and the powers are the figures that answer that. A reader who asks which figure is the real one has asked a question Paul does not ask."),
("sub", "1.3 The four questions"),
("p", "The volume puts four questions to every passage, in the same order. What does the passage say the Cross did? To what, or against what problem? For whom, as the passage words it? With what result? The questions are not a theory. They are a way of keeping each passage to its own sentences. They also make a disagreement testable. If a reader says that Galatians 3:13 teaches what Romans 3:25 teaches, the reader can be asked to show the same answers to the four questions in both."),
("ctbl", "The four questions.", [
    ("Question", "What it asks", "What it guards against"),
    ("What did the Cross do?", "The verb or figure the passage uses", "Replacing the passage’s word with another"),
    ("Against what?", "The problem the passage names", "Applying one passage’s problem to another"),
    ("For whom?", "The persons the passage names, in its own words", "Widening or narrowing the persons beyond the clause"),
    ("With what result?", "What follows, as the passage states it", "Adding a result the passage does not state"),
  ], [0.22, 0.40, 0.38]),
("sub", "1.4 What the chapter does not claim"),
("p", "The chapter does not claim that the passages cannot be set side by side. Chapter 14 does exactly that. It claims that they should be set side by side after each has been read, and not before. It does not claim that Paul is unconcerned with the unity of what Christ did. He writes that “God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself” (2 Corinthians 5:19) in the same chapter in which he says that the one died for all. The Cross is one event. The volume’s claim is that Paul describes what it did in more than one way, and that each way is given in a sentence that can be read."),
]
